<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Dillingen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1551</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of Dillingen was founded in 1551 by Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-truchsess-von-waldburg">Otto Truchsess von Waldburg</a>, prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, in his residence town of Dillingen on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Run by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> from 1563, it trained Catholic clergy for southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and became a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. It was reduced to a lyceum in 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-dillingen.jpg" alt="University of Dillingen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JohannesSch. (Serafim 89) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collegium_St_Hieronymi_Haupthaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
